50|UniProtKB:J7M799.\""},"featureId":"P0CU50.2:feature:13"},{"featureType":"Region","featureClass":"region","label":"Region of interest in the sequence","locationText":"163..181","start":163,"end":181,"segments":[{"start":163,"end":181}],"locationOperator":"simple","locationStatus":"supported","locationVersion":1,"qualifiers":{"region_name":"Region of interest in the sequence","note":"\"CAHS motif 2.","evidence":"ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:J7M799.\""},"featureId":"P0CU50.2:feature:14"},{"featureType":"Region","featureClass":"region","label":"Region of interest in the sequence","locationText":"205..227","start":205,"end":227,"segments":[{"start":205,"end":227}],"locationOperator":"simple","locationStatus":"supported","locationVersion":1,"qualifiers":{"region_name":"Region of interest in the sequence","note":"Disordered. /evidence=ECO:0000256|SAM:MobiDB-lite."},"featureId":"P0CU50.2:feature:15"},{"featureType":"Region","featureClass":"region","label":"Compositionally biased region","locationText":"205..219","start":205,"end":219,"segments":[{"start":205,"end":219}],"locationOperator":"simple","locationStatus":"supported","locationVersion":1,"qualifiers":{"region_name":"Compositionally biased region","note":"\"Low complexity.","evidence":"ECO:0000256|SAM:MobiDB-lite.\""},"featureId":"P0CU50.2:feature:16"}],"sites":[],"otherFeatures":[],"featureSummary":{"total":16,"sources":1,"genes":1,"proteins":1,"regions":13,"sites":0,"otherFeatures":0}},"rawText":"LOCUS       CAHS8_HYPEX              227 aa            linear   INV 02-SEP-2026\nDEFINITION  RecName: Full=Cytosolic-abundant heat soluble protein 94063;\n            Short=CAHS 94063; AltName: Full=Cytosolic abundant heat-soluble\n            protein 8; AltName: Full=Tardigrade-specific intrinsically\n            disordered protein CAHS 94063; Short=TDP CAHS 94063.\nACCESSION   P0CU50\nVERSION     P0CU50.2\nDBSOURCE    UniProtKB: locus CAHS8_HYPEX, accession P0CU50;\n            class: standard.\n            extra accessions:A0A1W0WK50\n            created: Jun 7, 2017.\n            sequence updated: Jan 28, 2026.\n            annotation updated: Sep 2, 2026.\n            xrefs: MTYJ01000087.1, OQV15590.1, 9RKP_A, 9RKP_B\n            xrefs (non-sequence databases): PDBsum:9RKP, AlphaFoldDB:P0CU50,\n            OrthoDB:10503333at2759, Proteomes:UP000192578, GO:0005737,\n            GO:0009269, DisProt:DP01384, DisProt:DP03738\nKEYWORDS    3D-structure; Coiled coil; Cytoplasm; Reference proteome; Repeat;\n            Stress response.\nSOURCE      Hypsibius exemplaris\n  ORGANISM  Hypsibius exemplaris\n            Eukaryota; Metazoa; Ecdysozoa; Tardigrada; Eutardigrada; Parachela;\n            Hypsibioidea; Hypsibiidae; Hypsibius.\nREFERENCE   1  (residues 1 to 227)\n  AUTHORS   Yoshida,Y., Koutsovoulos,G., Laetsch,D.R., Stevens,L., Kumar,S.,\n            Horikawa,D.D., Ishino,K., Komine,S., Kunieda,T., Tomita,M.,\n            Blaxter,M. and Arakawa,K.\n  TITLE     Comparative genomics of the tardigrades Hypsibius dujardini and\n            Ramazzottius varieornatus\n  JOURNAL   PLoS Biol 15 (7), e2002266 (2017)\n   PUBMED   28749982\n  REMARK    NUCLEOTIDE SEQUENCE [LARGE SCALE GENOMIC DNA].;\n            STRAIN=Z151\nREFERENCE   2  (residues 1 to 227)\n  AUTHORS   Boothby,T.C., Tapia,H., Brozena,A.H., Piszkiewicz,S., Smith,A.E.,\n            Giovannini,I., Rebecchi,L., Pielak,G.J., Koshland,D. and\n            Goldstein,B.\n  TITLE     Tardigrades Use Intrinsically Disordered Proteins to Survive\n            Desiccation\n  JOURNAL   Mol Cell 65 (6), 975-984 (2017)\n   PUBMED   28306513\n  REMARK    FUNCTION.\nREFERENCE   3  (residues 1 to 227)\n  AUTHORS   Arakawa,K. and Numata,K.\n  TITLE     Reconsidering the 'glass transition' hypothesis of intrinsically\n            unstructured CAHS proteins in desiccation tolerance of tardigrades\n  JOURNAL   Mol Cell 81 (3), 409-410 (2021)\n   PUBMED   33545053\n  REMARK    FUNCTION.\nREFERENCE   4  (residues 1 to 227)\n  AUTHORS   Malki,A., Teulon,J.M., Camacho-Zarco,A.R., Chen,S.W., Adamski,W.,\n            Maurin,D., Salvi,N., Pellequer,J.L. and Blackledge,M.\n  TITLE     Intrinsically Disordered Tardigrade Proteins Self-Assemble into\n            Fibrous Gels in Response to Environmental Stress\n  JOURNAL   Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 61, e202109961-e202109961 (2022)\n   PUBMED   34750927\n  REMARK    SUBUNIT.\nCOMMENT     On Jan 30, 2026 this sequence version replaced P0CU50.1.\n            [FUNCTION] CAHS proteins are cytosolic heat soluble proteins that\n            seem to contribute to the anhydrobiosis in tardigrades, but their\n            specific mechanisms are yet to be identified (PubMed:28306513,\n            PubMed:33545053). It is possible that protection during\n            anhydrobiosis might occur via the stabilization of vitrifying small\n            molecules such as sugars, but not via the direct glass transition\n            of CAHS proteins themselves (Probable).\n            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:28306513, ECO:0000269|PubMed:33545053,\n            ECO:0000305|PubMed:33545053}.\n            [SUBUNIT] At high concentrations, the monomers successively form\n            oligomers, long fibers and eventually a gel-like matrix in a\n            strongly temperature-dependent manner (PubMed:34750927). It may be\n            that under adverse environmental conditions, such as low\n            temperatures, the gel-like intracellular matrix can sequester\n            soluble proteins in their native state, maintaining their\n            functional form until environmental conditions become more\n            favourable (PubMed:34750927). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:34750927}.\n            [SUBCELLULAR LOCATION] Cytoplasm {ECO:0000305|PubMed:28306513}.\n            [DOMAIN] CAHS proteins contain 2 repeats of 19-mer peptides\n            designated as CAHS-motifs that comprise each two octapeptides\n            connected by a tripeptide (By similarity).\n            {ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:J7M799}.\n            [MISCELLANEOUS] Trehalose, a disaccharide essential for several\n            organisms to survive drying, is detected at low levels or not at\n            all in some tardigrade species, indicating that tardigrades possess\n            potentially novel mechanisms for surviving desiccation involving\n            tardigrade-specific intrinsically disordered proteins (TDPs)\n            (PubMed:28306513). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:28306513}.\n            [SIMILARITY] Belongs to the Cytosolic-abundant heat soluble protein\n            (CAHS) family. {ECO:0000305}.\n            [CAUTION] It was suggested that CAHS proteins were intrinsically\n            unstructured and show heat-dependent glass transition, which\n            contributes to the vitrification of cells, and this further leads\n            to desiccation tolerance (PubMed:28306513). However, more recent\n            studies led to the conclusion that there was no evidence supporting\n            glass transition of CAHS proteins to be contributing to the glass\n            transition of the whole tardigrade (PubMed:33545053).\n            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:28306513, ECO:0000269|PubMed:33545053}.\nFEATURES             Location/Qualifiers\n     source          1..227\n                     /organism=\"Hypsibius exemplaris\"\n                     /db_xref=\"taxon:2072580\"\n     gene            1..227\n                     /gene=\"CAHS 94063\"\n                     /locus_tag=\"BV898_10309\"\n                     /gene_synonym=\"CAHS-8\"\n     Protein         1..227\n                     /product=\"Cytosolic-abundant heat soluble protein 94063\"\n                     /note=\"CAHS 94063; Cytosolic abundant heat-soluble protein\n                     8; Tardigrade-specific intrinsically disordered protein\n                     CAHS 94063; TDP CAHS 94063\"\n                     /UniProtKB_evidence=\"Evidence at protein level\"\n     Region          1..227\n                     /region_name=\"Mature chain\"\n      